course: dictionary
lesson: 13264
title: Gnarled: twisted and knobbly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Gnarled is a describing word, an adjective. It tells you what something looks like. Gnarled means twisted, rough and full of knots, like an old tree trunk or branch. «The gnarled oak stood alone in the middle of the field.» An oak is a big tree, and gnarled tells you its trunk is twisted and knobbly, not smooth.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «The forest was dark and quiet, and the gnarled roots twisted across the path.» Dark, quiet and twisting roots give a spooky feeling. Gnarled tells you how the roots look and how the place feels.
explain: Gnarled can also describe people. «Grandma's gnarled hands kneaded the bread.» Her hands are bent and knobbly, often from years of hard work. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in a simpler word, like twisted or bent, and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Gnarled means twisted, rough and full of knots.
fact: «Gnarled» can describe trees, roots, branches, hands and fingers.
fact: When it describes hands or fingers, gnarled means bent and knobbly, often from old age or hard work.
fact: Gnarled is an adjective, and its comparative form is more gnarled.
fact: Gnarled trees and roots often make a story feel old, ancient or a little spooky.
exercise: «The gnarled oak had roots as thick as my arm.» What does gnarled mean here? | Green and full of leaves | Very tall and thin | *Twisted, rough and full of knots | Smooth and shiny like glass
exercise: «The old farmer's gnarled fingers gently fixed the fence.» What do the gnarled fingers show? | Painted in bright colours | Cold and wet from the rain | Long and thin like a pencil | *Bent and knobbly from hard work
exercise: «The gnarled branch creaked above the path.» Find and copy the word that means twisted and knobbly. | above | *gnarled | creaked | branch
test: «The witch's gnarled staff tapped on the stones.» What does gnarled tell you about the staff? | It is shiny and new | *It is twisted, rough and full of knots | It is light enough to float | It is painted gold
test: Ava says gnarled is just another word for old. Why is she only partly right? | Gnarled means very young | *Gnarled can mean old, but it mainly describes a twisted, knobbly shape and texture | It is only about trees | Gnarled means very loud
exam: Which sentence uses gnarled to describe an old, hard-working person? | «The gnarled puppy ran across the lawn.» | «The gnarled bicycle was brand new and shiny.» | *«His gnarled hands had dug the vegetable patch for fifty years.»
